# `const list = holder.nodes; .. list[i].kind`: of what the field holds Link proves the class, Array, and a type never proves contents. What the layout writes of them, `Node[]`, is expected of the list all the same:
# so a Node is expected of `list[i]`, a guess is opened, and `.kind` is a read of its slot. Taken for all that is expected, the proven class said nothing of the elements, and the read was closed: 57 instructions a turn against 25
# The list leaves the program, so that Link has seen nothing of what comes out of it (LinkTables::resultsOfReadsByKey).
# f1 = once, f2 = everyTurn: `holder.nodes[i]`. f3 = loose: `any[]` is written, which says nothing: there what the program was SEEN to put into the list is expected (LinkTables::resultsOfReadsByKey of Facts::held()):
# a guess with its test at the use, and the closed read behind the test: in the copy of the loop that other code may run in, one operation that does both. It is never a proof: the element itself stays `anything`.
